  "mcmc_samples_msc": "All 13 sources have a record (one row per source_id, samples in array columns). Sample bodies are not reproduced in this file.",
  "memo": "GSP-Phot percentile widths (84-16): A_G widths are 0.008-0.018 mag for most of the 10 objects that have GSP-Phot at all (#12: 0.018, #9: 0.008, #5: 0.014, #13: 0.015, #4: 0.012, #2: 0.014), with #7: 0.067, #6 BH1: 0.088, #3: 0.147 and the widest #11: 0.394. M_G widths: #11: 1.750, #6 BH1: 0.813, #3: 0.619, others 0.034-0.316. #1, #8, #10 have no GSP-Phot values at all (hence no percentiles). POSITION: these percentiles are the internal MCMC precision under the single-star model; they do NOT include the model systematic. The unresolved-binary model (MSC) gives A_G values that differ by far more than the percentile widths: #12 GSP-Phot 2.6713 vs MSC 0.0018 (difference -2.67 mag), #7: 0.5238 vs 0.0819, #6 BH1: 0.5575 vs 0.8966 (MSC interval itself is wide: 0.699-3.741). Therefore, when GSP-Phot A_G / M_G are used as fixed inputs to a binary model, the relevant uncertainty is the inter-model systematic (up to ~2.7 mag depending on the object), not the 0.01-0.4 mag percentile width. The +/-0.30 mag tolerance used in the beta scans is also small compared with this systematic. MSC AVAILABILITY: the MSC columns inside gaiadr3.astrophysical_parameters have values for all 13 objects, including #1, #8, #10 which lack GSP-Phot. gaiadr3.mcmc_samples_msc has one record per source for all 13 (samples stored in array columns; not reproduced in this file). gaiadr3.binary_masses contains 7 of the 13 (the NSS-solution objects #4, #6, #7, #8, #9, #10, #12) with masses from orbital solution + M1 (e.g. #6 BH1: m2 = 12.81 [10.49, 15.63]). CAVEAT: MSC itself rests on its own model assumptions (two main-sequence components etc.) and its A_G is not ground truth either (e.g. #4 ag_msc_upper sits at the prior edge 5.0). The realistic use is to record the GSP-Phot (single-star) vs MSC (binary) difference as the scale of the systematic, not to fix either one as truth."
}